# Frontend Development with Dummy API Routes

## Overview

During the initial frontend development phase, we use **dummy backend routes** that return hardcoded mock data. This allows frontend development to proceed rapidly without waiting for backend implementation.

**Key principles:**
- ✅ Use real API endpoints with dummy data
- ✅ Follow proper REST conventions
- ✅ Implement full error handling (even with dummy routes)
- ✅ Design for real backend integration later
- ✅ Document dummy data structure clearly

## Development Workflow

### Phase 1: API-First Design
1. **Define API contracts** in TypeScript interfaces
2. **Create dummy backend routes** returning mock data
3. **Build frontend components** using real API calls
4. **Test complete user flows** with dummy data

### Phase 2: Backend Integration (Later)
1. Backend team implements real logic
2. Frontend updates to use real data
3. Remove dummy route implementations
4. Update error handling for real scenarios

## Database Schema

This application uses the following database structure:

### Users Table
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique user identifier
- **email** (string) - User email address
- **password** (secretstring) - Hashed password
- **name** (string) - User's full name
- **picture** (png) - Profile picture
- **role** (string) - User role/permissions

### Cases Table (formerly "Request")
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique case identifier
- **title** (string) - Case title
- **description** (string) - Case description
- **password** (secretstring) - Case password/access control
- **document_IDs** (List\<UUID\>) - Array of associated document UUIDs

### Documents Table
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique document identifier
- **title** (string) - Document title
- **description** (string) - Document description
- **type** (string) - Document type/category
- **file_path** (string) - Storage path/URL for the PDF file
- **file_size** (integer) - File size in bytes
- **page_count** (integer) - Total number of pages in PDF
- **processing_status** (enum) - Processing status: "pending", "processing", "completed", "failed"
- **uploaded_at** (timestamp) - When the file was uploaded
- **processed_at** (timestamp) - When processing completed

### Chunks Table
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique chunk identifier
- **document_ID** (UUID, FK) - References Documents table
- **page** (integer) - Page number in document
- **context** (string) - Chunk text content
- **bbox** (bbox) - Bounding box coordinates

### ChunkStakeholders Table (Junction/Link Table)
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique identifier
- **chunk_ID** (UUID, FK) - References Chunks table
- **stakeholder_ID** (UUID, FK) - References Stakeholders table
- **confidence** (float) - Confidence score (0.0 - 1.0) for stakeholder identification
- **mention_text** (string) - Actual text where stakeholder was mentioned
- **mention_bbox** (bbox) - Bounding box of the specific mention within the chunk
- **created_at** (timestamp) - When the link was created
- **verified** (boolean) - Whether a human has verified this stakeholder identification

### Issues Table
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique issue identifier
- **chunk_stakeholder_ID** (UUID, FK) - References ChunkStakeholders table (links to specific stakeholder mention in chunk)
- **assigned_user_IDs** (List\<UUID\>) - Array of user UUIDs assigned to handle this stakeholder issue
- **status** (enum) - Issue status: "open", "in_progress", "resolved", "closed"
- **priority** (enum) - Priority level: "low", "medium", "high", "urgent"
- **description** (text) - Optional description of the issue
- **created_at** (timestamp) - When the issue was created
- **updated_at** (timestamp) - Last update timestamp
- **resolved_at** (timestamp) - When the issue was resolved

### Stakeholders Table
- **uuid** (UUID, PK) - Unique stakeholder identifier
- **type** (string) - Stakeholder type/category
- **name** (string) - Stakeholder name
- **contact_info** (string) - Contact information

### Relationships
- **Cases → Documents**: One-to-Many (Cases.document_IDs → Documents.uuid)
- **Documents → Chunks**: One-to-Many (Documents.uuid → Chunks.document_ID)
- **Chunks → Stakeholders**: Many-to-Many via ChunkStakeholders (with confidence scores)
- **ChunkStakeholders → Issues**: One-to-One (Each stakeholder mention in a chunk can have one issue)
- **Issues → Users**: Many-to-Many (Issues.assigned_user_IDs → Users.uuid)

### Important Clarifications

**Multiple Stakeholders in One Chunk:**
- If a paragraph (chunk) mentions 4 different stakeholders, this creates:
  - 1 Chunk record
  - 4 ChunkStakeholder records (one for each stakeholder mention with individual confidence scores)
  - Up to 4 Issue records (one per ChunkStakeholder, if issues are created for each)

**Example:**
```
Chunk: "The Environmental Agency and Local Council met with Community Group A 
        and Business Association B to discuss the project."

Creates:
- 1 Chunk (the entire paragraph)
- 4 ChunkStakeholder records:
  * Environmental Agency (confidence: 0.95)
  * Local Council (confidence: 0.92)
  * Community Group A (confidence: 0.88)
  * Business Association B (confidence: 0.85)
- 4 potential Issues (if action is needed for each stakeholder)
```

## Document Upload & Processing Workflow

This application includes a sophisticated PDF upload and processing pipeline that extracts text, identifies entities (stakeholders), and creates annotated chunks for analysis.

### Workflow Overview

```
1. User uploads PDF(s)
   ↓
2. Backend stores files and creates Document records (status: "pending")
   ↓
3. Background processing begins (status: "processing")
   - Extract text from PDF pages
   - Identify stakeholder entities (NER/pattern matching)
   - Create chunks with bounding boxes
   - Link chunks to stakeholders
   ↓
4. Processing completes (status: "completed")
   ↓
5. Frontend displays PDF with highlighted chunks
   - Color-coded by stakeholder type
   - Interactive highlights showing stakeholder info
   - Click to view chunk details/issues
```

